<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection"><a href="#A3.SS7" title="In Appendix C Proofs for guided DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref">C.7 </span>Proof of Lemma <span class="ltx_ref"></span></span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection"><a href="#A3.SS8" title="In Appendix C Proofs for guided DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref">C.8 </span>Proof of Lemma <span class="ltx_ref"></span></span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection"><a href="#A3.SS9" title="In Appendix C Proofs for guided DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref">C.9 </span>Proof of Lemma <span class="ltx_ref"></span></span></a></li>
</ol></li>
</ol></nav>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Score-based diffusion models</em>, originally inspired by thermodynamic principles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, have emerged as a powerful class of generative models, achieving state-of-the-art performance across a wide range of applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saharia et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Watson et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abramson et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
At a high level, diffusion models generate samples by progressively denoising a sequence of intermediate distributions <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby transforming a simple, easy-to-sample distribution <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., Gaussian noise) into a complex target distribution <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This generative procedure is driven by a sequence of <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">score functions</em> <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\nabla\log P_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\nabla\log P_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are typically learned by training deep neural networks through a score matching procedure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hyvärinen and Dayan, 2005</a>)</cite>.
Given the estimated score functions, diffusion models generate samples through an <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-step iterative process, which is illustrated by the following schema:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{1}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{sample}}Y_{1}\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{1}}Y_{2}\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{2}}\cdots\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{N-1}}Y_{N}\,\overset{d}{\approx}\,P_{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">sample</mo></mover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mrow></mover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mover><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>≈</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑑</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{1}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{sample}}Y_{1}\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{1}}Y_{2}\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{2}}\cdots\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{N-1}}Y_{N}\,\overset{d}{\approx}\,P_{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, many applications require controllable data generation to ensure that the generated content aligns with specific objectives or user-defined preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
A common approach to achieve this goal with diffusion models is <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">diffusion guidance</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans, 2022</a>)</cite>,
a technique that steers the generation process toward samples with the desired attributes.
Specifically, to generate samples associated with a target label <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, diffusion guidance modifies the unconditional score <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log P_{i}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log P_{i}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> by incorporating an additional guidance term <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma\nabla\log P_{i}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\nabla\log P_{i}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N-1]</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the strength of the guidance.
In practice, this guidance term must be learned from data, and the two main approaches for doing so are <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">classifier guidance</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>)</cite> and <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">classifier-free guidance</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans, 2022</a>)</cite>, depending on whether the term is approximated via classifier training or joint score estimation.
Notably, diffusion guidance not only enables controllable generation, but also often improves the quality of the generated samples.
An informal illustration of the sample generation process under guided diffusion models is provided below, and a more detailed description is deferred to Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. From a theoretical perspective, both guided and unguided diffusion models can be understood as discrete approximations of continuous-time processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>.</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{1}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{sample}}Y_{1}\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{1}+{\color[rgb]{0.72,0,0}\mathrm{guidance}}}Y_{2}\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{2}+{\color[rgb]{0.72,0,0}\mathrm{guidance}}}\cdots\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{N-1}+{\color[rgb]{0.72,0,0}\mathrm{guidance}}}Y_{N}\,\overset{d}{\approx}\,{\color[rgb]{0.72,0,0}\mathrm{?}}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">sample</mo></mover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#B80000;" mathcolor="#B80000" mathsize="0.700em">guidance</mi></mrow></mover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#B80000;" mathcolor="#B80000" mathsize="0.700em">guidance</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#B80000;" mathcolor="#B80000" mathsize="0.700em">guidance</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>≈</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑑</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#B80000;" mathcolor="#B80000">?</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{1}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{sample}}Y_{1}\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{1}+{\color[rgb]{0.72,0,0}\mathrm{guidance}}}Y_{2}\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{2}+{\color[rgb]{0.72,0,0}\mathrm{guidance}}}\cdots\xrightarrow{\nabla\log P_{N-1}+{\color[rgb]{0.72,0,0}\mathrm{guidance}}}Y_{N}\,\overset{d}{\approx}\,{\color[rgb]{0.72,0,0}\mathrm{?}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite its empirical success, the theoretical properties of diffusion guidance remain largely unexplored.
In particular, the distribution produced by guided diffusion sampling has not yet been fully characterized.
To shed light on the effectiveness of diffusion guidance, a line of work analyzes simplified settings in which the target distribution admits tractable structure, for example, when the conditional distributions are Gaussian or supported on one-dimensional intervals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chidambaram et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ventura et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
For more general distributions, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley and Nakkiran (2024)</a></cite> demonstrate that guidance does not, in general, sample from a powered distribution, and can be interpreted as a predictor-corrector method.
Recent work establishes an average-case result demonstrating that diffusion guidance reduces the expected reciprocal of the conditional label probability,
and thus increases classification confidence in an average-case sense <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Jiao, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
From a different perspective, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahu et al. (2026)</a></cite> show that under mild smoothness conditions, classifiers trained by minimizing the empirical cross-entropy loss lead to an effective guidance mechanism.
For conditional sample generation with hard constraints, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2026)</a></cite> propose a guidance framework based on Doob’s <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we adopt a different perspective, explaining the success of diffusion guidance through the <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">robustness of support</em> property <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chandramoorthy and de Clercq, 2025</a>)</cite>.
In particular, we ask the following question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:276.0pt;"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Does diffusion guidance generate samples that are close to the target support?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The ability to generate samples near the target support is a highly desirable property for generative models, since off-support samples are often structurally implausible and can adversely impact downstream tasks.
For example, a recent paper on video sampling empirically demonstrates that unmodified generative models could fail to achieve the robustness of support property and produce physically distorted videos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
In the context of diffusion guidance, this means that although guidance may not generate samples exactly from the conditional distribution associated with the label of interest (and indeed should not when <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>),
we still expect it to produce physically meaningful and plausible samples that remain near the target support.
Support robustness of diffusion guidance has been established in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chidambaram et al. (2024)</a></cite> for one-dimensional mixture distributions.
However, it is unclear whether their analysis extends to high-dimensional settings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Main contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we study the behavior of diffusion guidance when the target distribution is a mixture of general distributions supported on compact convex sets.
Our findings are summarized below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Support robustness with perfect score estimation: </span>
We show that guided diffusion models with exact access to the score functions exhibit strong support robustness, in the sense that the generated sample paths almost surely converge to the correct support.
Our theory applies broadly to all guidance strength <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and to a wide family of discretization schemes induced by an exponential integrator (including continuous-time processes with infinitesimal discretization), and covers both the deterministic diffusion sampler Denoising Diffusion Implicit Models (DDIM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>
and the stochastic diffusion sampler Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
Our theory implies that, in the absence of score estimation error and with sufficiently fine discretization, diffusion guidance with arbitrary guidance strength produces physically plausible samples that remain close to the support of the target component.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Numerical evidence: </span>
We provide numerical evidence supporting our theoretical findings. Based on experimental outcomes, we further conjecture that, under mild regularity assumptions, convergence to the target support may continue to hold even in the absence of support convexity.
We leave a rigorous investigation of this point for future work.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our results provide insight into the qualitative behavior of the sample paths produced by diffusion guidance, revealing that the resulting dynamics inherently adapt to the intrinsic geometry of the target distribution.
In practice, however, diffusion guidance can still produce physically implausible samples, particularly when the guidance strength <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is large.
Our results suggest that this behavior may stem either from errors in score estimation or from an insufficiently fine discretization scheme.
Note that diffusion models are generally expected to fail under inaccurate score estimation or coarse discretization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Related works</h3>

<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theoretical explanations for the success of diffusion guidance.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A recent line of work seeks to theoretically explain the empirical success of diffusion guidance.
Among others, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024a)</a></cite> analyzes diffusion guidance when the target distribution is a Gaussian mixture model (GMM).
Specifically, they show that applying guidance to both DDIM and DDPM increases classification confidence, defined as the posterior probability of the label of interest.
Moreover, they prove that for guided DDIM, diffusion guidance decreases differential entropy, theoretically validating the empirical observation that guidance reduces sample diversity.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chidambaram et al. (2024)</a></cite> analyzes guided DDIM applied to GMM or mixture distributions supported on disjoint intervals.
Their results show that strong guidance steers the generated samples toward the boundary of the target component.
More recently, several works have studied diffusion guidance for general distributions without an explicit parametric form <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley and Nakkiran, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Jiao, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Since we have discussed these works earlier, we omit further discussion here.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward-guided diffusion models. </h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Another application of controllable generation with diffusion models is reward-guided diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wallace et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, where the objective is to generate samples that achieve higher reward values. These methods modify the reverse diffusion dynamics by incorporating gradients of a reward function, thereby steering the generation process toward outputs that receive higher reward.
Here, the reward function might represent various objectives, including alignment with human preferences or adherence to given constraints.
We refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Uehara et al. (2025)</a></cite> for a review of recent developments in this area.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other theoretical advances for diffusion models.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Various results have been established to explain the overall success of diffusion models,
covering topics including convergence rates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Yan, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, score estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wibisono et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and applications in statistical or optimization settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Montanari and Wu, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
We refer interested readers to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a></cite> for a summary of recent theoretical developments in the past few years.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Organization</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S2" title="2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we provide a brief overview of diffusion models and diffusion guidance.
In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we present and prove the main results for DDIM, while Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the corresponding results for DDPM.
We provide intuition for our theoretical results in the main text, while deferring the complete proofs to the appendix.
Numerical experiments are reported in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
We conclude the paper and discuss several directions for future work in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Discussion ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4 </span>Notation</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the set <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d]=\{1,2,\cdots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d]=\{1,2,\cdots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a probability measure <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{supp}(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{supp}(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> the support of <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unless otherwise specified, the symbol <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\cdot\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\cdot\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> will denote the usual Euclidean <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-norm.
For a set <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and a vector <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dist}(x,A)=\inf_{y\in A}\left\|x-y\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗂𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dist}(x,A)=\inf_{y\in A}\left\|x-y\right\|</annotation></semantics></math>.
The (set-valued) projection operator is defined by <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Proj_{A}(x)=\argmin_{y\in A}\left\|x-y\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖯𝗋𝗈𝗃</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">argmin</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Proj_{A}(x)=\argmin_{y\in A}\left\|x-y\right\|</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Proj_{A}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖯𝗋𝗈𝗃</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Proj_{A}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> contains a single element, then we denote that element by <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Proj_{A}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖯𝗋𝗈𝗃</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Proj_{A}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> the unit sphere in <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> centered at the origin.
For a set <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cl}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cl}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> the closure of <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{int}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗂𝗇𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{int}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> the interior of <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vol(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗏𝗈𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vol(K)</annotation></semantics></math> the volume of <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>,
and define the boundary of <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial K=\mathsf{cl}(K)\backslash\mathsf{int}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mi>𝗂𝗇𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial K=\mathsf{cl}(K)\backslash\mathsf{int}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}(x,\delta)=\{y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}:\|y-x\|&lt;\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}(x,\delta)=\{y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}:\|y-x\|&lt;\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Score-based diffusion models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides a brief overview of diffusion models.
At a high level, a diffusion model consists of a <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forward process</em> and a <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reverse process</em>.
In the forward process, the target distribution is progressively transformed into an easy-to-sample noise distribution (typically a standard Gaussian).
The reverse process, in turn, maps this noise back to the target distribution, enabling the generation of new samples.
Note that only the reverse process is used during the sample generation stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models are often analyzed through their continuous-time limits, and are typically implemented as numerical approximations to these limits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>.
For better understanding, we first introduce the continuous-time formulation of diffusion models, and then describe the samplers implemented in practice, which can be viewed as time-discretized approximations of these continuous-time processes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A common choice for the forward process is based on the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process.
Specifically, let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the target distribution to be sampled. We then consider the following stochastic differential equation (SDE) defined on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}y_{t}=-y_{t}\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\,\mathrm{d}W_{t},\qquad y_{0}\sim p_{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}y_{t}=-y_{t}\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\,\mathrm{d}W_{t},\qquad y_{0}\sim p_{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W_{t})_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W_{t})_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a standard <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued Wiener process that is independent of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the distribution of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Standard computation implies that</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;p_{t}\overset{d}{=}\lambda_{t}\,y_{0}+\sigma_{t}\,w,\qquad(y_{0},w)\sim p_{0}\otimes\pi_{d},\\
&amp;\lambda_{t}=e^{-t},\qquad\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{1-\lambda_{t}^{2}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑑</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;p_{t}\overset{d}{=}\lambda_{t}\,y_{0}+\sigma_{t}\,w,\qquad(y_{0},w)\sim p_{0}\otimes\pi_{d},\\
&amp;\lambda_{t}=e^{-t},\qquad\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{1-\lambda_{t}^{2}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the distribution of a standard Gaussian random vector in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
From <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we see that process (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) begins with the target distribution, and progressively injects Gaussian noise until the resulting distribution approaches a standard Gaussian one.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the reverse process, two commonly used approaches are DDIM and DDPM.
Their continuous-time formulations corresponding to the forward process (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) are given below:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DDIM sampler. </em>
The continuous-time process underlying the deterministic DDIM sampler is formulated as an ordinary differential equation (ODE) on <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(x_{t}+\nabla\log p_{T-t}(x_{t})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t,\qquad x_{0}\sim p_{T}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(x_{t}+\nabla\log p_{T-t}(x_{t})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t,\qquad x_{0}\sim p_{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The marginal distributions of process (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 1st item ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) match that of the forward diffusion process (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), in the sense that <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\overset{d}{=}y_{T-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑑</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\overset{d}{=}y_{T-t}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DDPM sampler. </em> The continuous-time process underlying the stochastic DDPM sampler is on the other hand formulated as an SDE defined on <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(x_{t}+2\nabla\log p_{T-t}(x_{t})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\,\mathrm{d}B_{t}\qquad x_{0}\sim p_{T}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(x_{t}+2\nabla\log p_{T-t}(x_{t})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\,\mathrm{d}B_{t}\qquad x_{0}\sim p_{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{t})_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{t})_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Wiener process that is independent of <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Process (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2nd item ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) also matches the marginal distributions of process (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in a time-reversed sense: <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\overset{d}{=}y_{T-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑑</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\overset{d}{=}y_{T-t}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anderson, 1982</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Processes (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 1st item ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2nd item ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) are both driven by a family of <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">score functions</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\nabla\log p_{T-t}\}_{0\leq t&lt;T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\nabla\log p_{T-t}\}_{0\leq t&lt;T}</annotation></semantics></math>, which in practice are unknown and are typically learned from training data drawn from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> through a <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">score matching</em> procedure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hyvärinen and Dayan, 2005</a>)</cite>.
In addition, the correct initial distribution <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> hence is also unknown.
On the positive side, from <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we know that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> can be well approximated by a standard Gaussian distribution for only moderately large <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (See Proposition 4 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al. (2024)</a></cite> for a rigorous justification of this point).
To sample from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than tracking the processes in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 1st item ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2nd item ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) all the way to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, practitioners typically stop early at <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> for a small positive <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid numerical instability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion models via time-discretization.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Apart from score estimation and approximate initialization,
practical implementation of DDIM (DDPM) requires choosing a discretization scheme for the associated reverse-time ODE (SDE).
There are multiple ways to discretize the reverse dynamics, and the choice of discretization scheme can significantly affect the resulting diffusion model.
For instance, a popular approach is the exponential integrator, which has been empirically shown to produce high-fidelity samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Chen, 2023</a>)</cite>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al. (2022)</a></cite> further improve the sampling efficiency of diffusion models by adaptively selecting discretization step sizes.
Another line of work investigates the use of high-order numerical solvers to accelerate sample generation with diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
For a summary of other common discretization strategies, we refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>, Section 2)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Controllable generation with diffusion guidance</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In many practical scenarios, instead of sampling unconditionally from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, one may wish to generate samples associated with a user-specified label <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Diffusion guidance achieves this by augmenting the unconditional score <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{T-t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{T-t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> with an additional term <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the hyperparameter <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the guidance strength.
Here, with a slight abuse of notation, we denote by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the joint distribution of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{t},\eta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{t},\eta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming that the Wiener process <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W_{t})_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W_{t})_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.E1" title="In Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is independent of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{0},\eta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{0},\eta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
From a continuous-time perspective, the guided ODE associated with the DDIM sampler takes the following form:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\mathrm{d}x_{t}=&amp;\left(x_{t}+\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(\eta_{0}\mid x_{t})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t\\
=&amp;\left(x_{t}+(1-\gamma)\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t},\eta_{0})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.340em">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.340em">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\mathrm{d}x_{t}=&amp;\left(x_{t}+\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(\eta_{0}\mid x_{t})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t\\
=&amp;\left(x_{t}+(1-\gamma)\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t},\eta_{0})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T-t}(\eta_{0}\mid x_{t})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T-t}(\eta_{0}\mid x_{t})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability of label <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T-t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Throughout this work, unless otherwise stated, all gradients are taken with respect to the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-space.
Similarly, the guided SDE associated with the DDPM sampler admits the following expression:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\mathrm{d}x_{t}=&amp;\left(x_{t}+2\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t})+2\gamma\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(\eta_{0}\mid x_{t})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\,\mathrm{d}B_{t}\\
=&amp;\left(x_{t}+2(1-\gamma)\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t})+2\gamma\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t},\eta_{0})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\mathrm{d}B_{t}.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.340em">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.340em">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\mathrm{d}x_{t}=&amp;\left(x_{t}+2\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t})+2\gamma\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(\eta_{0}\mid x_{t})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\,\mathrm{d}B_{t}\\
=&amp;\left(x_{t}+2(1-\gamma)\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t})+2\gamma\nabla\log p_{{T-t}}(x_{t},\eta_{0})\right)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\mathrm{d}B_{t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In practice, the additional guidance term must also be learned from data. Depending on the training approach, it can be classified as either classifier guidance (where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{T-t}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T-t}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is learned) or classifier-free guidance (where a joint score function over both the label and the sample space is learned).
We refer interested readers to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol (2021)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans (2022)</a></cite> for further details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that when <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>, processes (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) reduce to the unconditional sampling processes (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 1st item ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2nd item ‣ Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. ‣ 2.1 Score-based diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), respectively. On the other hand, when <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, they coincide with the reverse processes associated with the conditional distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{0}(\cdot\mid\eta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\cdot\mid\eta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notably, practitioners often choose <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> to improve fidelity of the generated samples. With this choice, the goal is not to sample exactly from <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{0}(\cdot\mid\eta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\cdot\mid\eta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, but to generate samples that are more strongly aligned with the target label <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Results for DDIM</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our main results for DDIM.
Throughout this paper, we denote by <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> a finite set of labels and,
without loss of generality, assume that diffusion guidance is always directed toward samples associated with <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Summary of main results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by stating the assumptions on the target distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> required to establish our main theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmass1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ass">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmass1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmass1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmass1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmass1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmass1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We assume that <math id="S3.Thmass1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}p_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}p_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is a mixture distribution in <math id="S3.Thmass1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmass1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{\eta})_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{\eta})_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-negative mixture weights that satisfy <math id="S3.Thmass1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmass1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability density function supported on a convex and compact set <math id="S3.Thmass1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We further impose the following assumptions:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The set </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains at least one interior point for all </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The weight associated with the target component is positive, i.e., </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\eta_{0}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\eta_{0}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The density function associated with the target component is bounded from below on its support, i.e., </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{x\in K_{\eta_{0}}}p_{\eta_{0}}(x)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{x\in K_{\eta_{0}}}p_{\eta_{0}}(x)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For simplicity, we first present our findings for the continuous-time guided ODE.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t})_{0\leq t&lt;T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t})_{0\leq t&lt;T}</annotation></semantics></math> be any sample path that represents a solution to the guided ODE (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), then under Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmass1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, for any starting location <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and guidance strength <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lim_{t\to T^{-}}\mathsf{dist}(x_{t},K_{\eta_{0}})=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗂𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lim_{t\to T^{-}}\mathsf{dist}(x_{t},K_{\eta_{0}})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Proofs for guided DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmrem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmrem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmrem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmrem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmrem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> establishes a path-wise convergence result that holds for any initial location, providing stronger guarantees than those based on average-case analysis.
Our results show that, for a sufficiently small early-stopping parameter <math id="S3.Thmrem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the samples generated by the guided ODE (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) remain within the vicinity of the target support.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We then extend the continuous-time analysis given in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to cover the discrete samplers used in practice.
While we focus on the sampling scheme based on the exponential integrator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Chen, 2023</a>)</cite>, we expect that our results generalize to other discretization methods as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To describe the sampling scheme based on the exponential integrator, we consider a discretization with time steps <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=t_{0}&lt;t_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;t_{N}\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=t_{0}&lt;t_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;t_{N}\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>.
On each interval <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{k},t_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{k},t_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,1,\ldots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,1,\ldots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we perform the following update</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\widehat{x}_{t}=\big(\widehat{x}_{t}+\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\eta_{0}\mid\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})\big)\,\mathrm{d}t." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\widehat{x}_{t}=\big(\widehat{x}_{t}+\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\eta_{0}\mid\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})\big)\,\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Update equation (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is derived by replacing the non-linear drift term <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla\log p_{T-t}(\cdot)+\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{T-t}(\cdot)+\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> with its value at <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> on the interval <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{k},t_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{k},t_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that this update equation can be expressed in the following closed form:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\widehat{x}_{t_{k+1}}=e^{\Delta_{k}}\widehat{x}_{t_{k}}+(e^{\Delta_{k}}-1)\cdot\big(\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\eta_{0}\mid\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})\big),\\
&amp;\Delta_{k}=t_{k+1}-t_{k},\qquad k=0,1,\cdots,N-1.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\widehat{x}_{t_{k+1}}=e^{\Delta_{k}}\widehat{x}_{t_{k}}+(e^{\Delta_{k}}-1)\cdot\big(\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\eta_{0}\mid\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})\big),\\
&amp;\Delta_{k}=t_{k+1}-t_{k},\qquad k=0,1,\cdots,N-1.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">We next impose an assumption on the step sizes.
Note that our assumption is standard and aligns with the one adopted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmass2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ass">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmass2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmass2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmass2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmass2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmass2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We assume that there exists <math id="S3.Thmass2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S3.Thmass2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{k}\leq\kappa\min\{1,T-t_{k+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k}\leq\kappa\min\{1,T-t_{k+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For the exponential integrator updating scheme (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), we have the following theorem.
We expect similar results to hold for other discretization schemes, and here we analyze one that is commonly used.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmass1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmass2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
there exists <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> that depends only on <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{0},\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{0},\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for all <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\kappa\leq\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\kappa\leq\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following statement is true:
for any discretization scheme <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{k}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{k}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;t_{k+1}&gt;t_{k}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;t_{k+1}&gt;t_{k}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lim_{k\to\infty}\mathsf{dist}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}},K_{\eta_{0}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗂𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lim_{k\to\infty}\mathsf{dist}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}},K_{\eta_{0}})=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for any initialization <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{t_{0}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{t_{0}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and guidance strength <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, the sequence <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\widehat{x}_{t_{k}}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\widehat{x}_{t_{k}}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is produced by <a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Proofs for guided DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmrem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmrem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmrem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmrem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmrem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far, we have considered a simplified setting in which the score functions and guidance terms are known exactly.
Evidently, the support vicinity property continues to hold under uniform bounds on the score and guidance term errors.
However, we do not yet know how to extend the result to the more realistic setting in which the errors are measured in <math id="S3.Thmrem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, it is natural to assume that the errors are under the base measures <math id="S3.Thmrem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{t})_{0&lt;t\leq T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{t})_{0&lt;t\leq T}</annotation></semantics></math> from the forward process, as both the score and the guidance terms are trained using data drawn from these distributions.
However, in the guided diffusion model, the intermediate states in the reverse process do not approximately follow the distributions of the forward process. Instead, their distributions depend on the choice of the guidance parameter <math id="S3.Thmrem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
This distribution mismatch represents the primary obstacle to extending our results to account for score and guidance estimation errors.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmrem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmrem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmrem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmrem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmrem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> requires the discretization scheme to be sufficiently fine, as governed by <math id="S3.Thmrem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, a closer inspection of our proof shows that the interval of <math id="S3.Thmrem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for which the theorem holds may shrink as <math id="S3.Thmrem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> increases,
indicating that a large guidance strength combined with insufficiently fine discretization can lead to physically distorted samples.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our main result <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> assumes that the support domains of the mixture components are convex.
It is worth noting that this convexity requirement may be somewhat restrictive, as practical mixture distributions do not necessarily exhibit convex support.
We make two remarks regarding this point.
First, our numerical experiments indicate that our theoretical findings continue to hold for mixture distributions with non-convex support.
Second, although many high-dimensional datasets in practice may not have convex support, their latent representations in a lower-dimensional subspace often form simpler, and sometimes even linearly separable clusters.
For instance, this effect is evident in the Principal Component Analysis (PCA) plots of human genomics data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elhaik (2022)</a></cite>.
On this note, it is worth noting that Latent Diffusion Models introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al. (2022)</a></cite>
have been shown to yield highly competitive performance on various image-related tasks compared to diffusion models trained directly on pixel space while being more computationally efficient.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present a change-of-variables reformulation of the process (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) that simplifies the analysis and offers intuition for the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;t\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;t\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>, we can express the density functions <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> using Gaussian convolutions:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t}(x)=\int\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t}(x)=\int\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid x)=\frac{w_{\eta_{0}}\int p_{\eta_{0}}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid x)=\frac{w_{\eta_{0}}\int p_{\eta_{0}}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi_{t}(z)=\frac{1}{(\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma_{t})^{d}}\exp\Big(-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}\|z\|^{2}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi_{t}(z)=\frac{1}{(\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma_{t})^{d}}\exp\Big(-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}\|z\|^{2}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Taking the gradients of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain by Stein’s formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stein, 1981</a>)</cite> the following expressions for the score function and the guidance term:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla\log p_{t}(x)=\frac{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})(-\sigma_{t}^{-2}x+\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla\log p_{t}(x)=\frac{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})(-\sigma_{t}^{-2}x+\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla\log p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid x)=\frac{\int p_{\eta_{0}}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})(-\sigma_{t}^{-2}x+\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\int p_{\eta_{0}}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla\log p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid x)=\frac{\int p_{\eta_{0}}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})(-\sigma_{t}^{-2}x+\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\int p_{\eta_{0}}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad-\frac{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})(-\sigma_{t}^{-2}x+\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad-\frac{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})(-\sigma_{t}^{-2}x+\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">For notational simplicity, for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;t\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;t\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}(x)=\frac{w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}=p_{t}(Y=\eta\mid X_{t}=x),\\
&amp;\overline{m}_{\eta,t}(x)=\frac{\int x_{0}p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}=\mathbb{E}\big[X_{0}\mid X_{t}=x,\,Y=\eta\big],\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}(x)=\frac{w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}=p_{t}(Y=\eta\mid X_{t}=x),\\
&amp;\overline{m}_{\eta,t}(x)=\frac{\int x_{0}p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(x-\lambda_{t}x_{0})\mathrm{d}x_{0}}=\mathbb{E}\big[X_{0}\mid X_{t}=x,\,Y=\eta\big],\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(Y=\eta)=w_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(Y=\eta)=w_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\mid Y=\eta\sim p_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\mid Y=\eta\sim p_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=\lambda_{t}X_{0}+\sigma_{t}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=\lambda_{t}X_{0}+\sigma_{t}Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\sim\mathcal{N}(0,{\rm I}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\sim\mathcal{N}(0,{\rm I}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Observe that for all <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{t}(x)=-\sigma_{t}^{-2}x+\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}(x)\overline{m}_{\eta,t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{t}(x)=-\sigma_{t}^{-2}x+\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}(x)\overline{m}_{\eta,t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid x)=\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}\overline{m}_{\eta_{0},t}(x)-\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}(x)\overline{m}_{\eta,t}(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{t}(\eta_{0}\mid x)=\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}\overline{m}_{\eta_{0},t}(x)-\lambda_{t}\sigma_{t}^{-2}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}(x)\overline{m}_{\eta,t}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Plugging the above expressions into the guided ODE (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), we conclude that</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\Big\{\frac{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}x_{t}+\frac{\gamma\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\overline{m}_{\eta_{0},T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{(\gamma-1)\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})\overline{m}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})\Big\}\mathrm{d}t\\
&amp;=\Big\{x_{t}+\frac{\gamma\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\Big(\overline{m}_{\eta_{0},T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{x_{t}}{\lambda_{T-t}}\Big)-\frac{(\gamma-1)\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})\Big(\overline{m}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{x_{t}}{\lambda_{T-t}}\Big)\Big\}\mathrm{d}t.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\Big\{\frac{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}x_{t}+\frac{\gamma\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\overline{m}_{\eta_{0},T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{(\gamma-1)\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})\overline{m}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})\Big\}\mathrm{d}t\\
&amp;=\Big\{x_{t}+\frac{\gamma\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\Big(\overline{m}_{\eta_{0},T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{x_{t}}{\lambda_{T-t}}\Big)-\frac{(\gamma-1)\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})\Big(\overline{m}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{x_{t}}{\lambda_{T-t}}\Big)\Big\}\mathrm{d}t.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.6" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=\lambda_{T-t}^{-1}x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=\lambda_{T-t}^{-1}x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.2 Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, we see that the resulting process <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{t})_{0\leq t&lt;T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{t})_{0\leq t&lt;T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the solution to the following ODE:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}z_{t}=\Big\{\frac{\gamma}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\left(m_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})-z_{t}\right)-\frac{\gamma-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\zeta_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})\left(m_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})-z_{t}\right)\Big\}\mathrm{d}t," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}z_{t}=\Big\{\frac{\gamma}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\left(m_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})-z_{t}\right)-\frac{\gamma-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\zeta_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})\left(m_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})-z_{t}\right)\Big\}\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.7" class="ltx_p">where for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\zeta_{\eta,t}(z)=\frac{w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(\lambda_{t}(x_{0}-z))\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(\lambda_{t}(x_{0}-z))\mathrm{d}x_{0}}=p_{t}(Y=\eta\mid X_{t}=\lambda_{t}z),\\
&amp;m_{\eta,t}(z)=\frac{\int x_{0}p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(\lambda_{t}(x_{0}-z))\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(\lambda_{t}(x_{0}-z))\mathrm{d}x_{0}}=\mathbb{E}\big[X_{0}\mid X_{t}=\lambda_{t}z,\,Y=\eta\big].\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\zeta_{\eta,t}(z)=\frac{w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(\lambda_{t}(x_{0}-z))\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}w_{\eta}\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(\lambda_{t}(x_{0}-z))\mathrm{d}x_{0}}=p_{t}(Y=\eta\mid X_{t}=\lambda_{t}z),\\
&amp;m_{\eta,t}(z)=\frac{\int x_{0}p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(\lambda_{t}(x_{0}-z))\mathrm{d}x_{0}}{\int p_{\eta}(x_{0})\phi_{t}(\lambda_{t}(x_{0}-z))\mathrm{d}x_{0}}=\mathbb{E}\big[X_{0}\mid X_{t}=\lambda_{t}z,\,Y=\eta\big].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.8" class="ltx_p">By definition, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\zeta_{\eta,t}(z)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\zeta_{\eta,t}(z)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the vector-valued function <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\eta,t}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\eta,t}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as the mean of a tilted version of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the tilt determined by the input vector <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, we know that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\eta,t}(z)\in K_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\eta,t}(z)\in K_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For notational convenience, for <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;t\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;t\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F_{\eta,t}(z)=m_{\eta,t}(z)-z." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F_{\eta,t}(z)=m_{\eta,t}(z)-z.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">With the above definition, we can re-write ODE (<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 3.2 Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}z_{t}=\Big\{\frac{\gamma}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}F_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})-\frac{\gamma-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\zeta_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})F_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})\Big\}\mathrm{d}t." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}z_{t}=\Big\{\frac{\gamma}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}F_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})-\frac{\gamma-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\zeta_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})F_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})\Big\}\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, one can interpret <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\eta,t}(z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\eta,t}(z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> as a “force” that pulls <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> towards a vector <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\eta,t}(z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\eta,t}(z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, which lies within <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the only acting force is <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, and one should therefore expect that the trajectory of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to move toward <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> increases.
When <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the trajectory is additionally subject to forces that repel it from other regions, and it is therefore not a priori clear that it will still move toward <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Nevertheless, as the analysis in the following sections shows, the force directed toward <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> dominates the other forces, thereby ensuring that the trajectory of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> ultimately converges to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
See Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2 Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for an illustration of ODE (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 3.2 Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07220v1/diffusion_guidance.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/177;" width="286" height="177" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of ODE (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 3.2 Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) when <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=\{\eta_{0},\eta_{1},\eta_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=\{\eta_{0},\eta_{1},\eta_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, guidance is towards the blue circle with label <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}=\frac{\gamma-(\gamma-1)\zeta_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}F_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}=\frac{\gamma-(\gamma-1)\zeta_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}F_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}=-\frac{\gamma-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\zeta_{\eta_{1},T-t}(z_{t})F_{\eta_{1},T-t}(z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}=-\frac{\gamma-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\zeta_{\eta_{1},T-t}(z_{t})F_{\eta_{1},T-t}(z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}=-\frac{\gamma-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\zeta_{\eta_{2},T-t}(z_{t})F_{\eta_{2},T-t}(z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}=-\frac{\gamma-1}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\zeta_{\eta_{2},T-t}(z_{t})F_{\eta_{2},T-t}(z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The combined force is given by <math id="S3.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm comb}=F_{0}+F_{1}+F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>comb</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm comb}=F_{0}+F_{1}+F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results for DDPM</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we extend our deterministic results in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to the stochastic setting, and establish the support robustness property for DDPM.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Summary of main results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the main results for the DDPM sampler in this section, covering both the continuous-time formulation and a discrete-time scheme based on an exponential integrator.
We start with the continuous-time result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t})_{0\leq t&lt;T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t})_{0\leq t&lt;T}</annotation></semantics></math> be any sample path that represents a solution to the guided SDE (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), then under Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmass1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, for any starting location <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and guidance strength <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, almost surely we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{t\to T^{-}}\mathsf{dist}(x_{t},K_{\eta_{0}})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗂𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{t\to T^{-}}\mathsf{dist}(x_{t},K_{\eta_{0}})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Summary of main results ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Summary of main results ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Proofs for guided DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the update rule defined in <a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we apply an exponential integrator scheme to DDPM with guidance.
Specifically, with time steps <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=t_{0}&lt;t_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;t_{N}&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=t_{0}&lt;t_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;t_{N}&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>,
we perform the following updates on the interval <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{k},t_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{k},t_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,1,\ldots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,1,\ldots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\widehat{x}_{t}=\big(\widehat{x}_{t}+2\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})+2\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\eta_{0}\mid\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})\big)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\mathrm{d}B_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\widehat{x}_{t}=\big(\widehat{x}_{t}+2\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})+2\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\eta_{0}\mid\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})\big)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\mathrm{d}B_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We next show that, almost surely, the sample path defined by process (<a href="#S4.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 4.1 Summary of main results ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) enters the target support <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the update rule defined in <a href="#S4.E15" title="In 4.1 Summary of main results ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> admits the following explicit form:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\widehat{x}_{t_{k+1}}=e^{\Delta_{k}}\widehat{x}_{t_{k}}+2(e^{\Delta_{k}}-1)\big(\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\eta_{0}\mid\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})\big)+\sqrt{e^{2\Delta_{k}}-1}\,Z_{k},\\
&amp;\Delta_{k}=t_{k+1}-t_{k},\qquad Z_{k}\sim_{i.i.d.}\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d}),\qquad k=0,1,\cdots,N-1.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\widehat{x}_{t_{k+1}}=e^{\Delta_{k}}\widehat{x}_{t_{k}}+2(e^{\Delta_{k}}-1)\big(\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})+\gamma\nabla\log p_{T-t_{k}}(\eta_{0}\mid\widehat{x}_{t_{k}})\big)+\sqrt{e^{2\Delta_{k}}-1}\,Z_{k},\\
&amp;\Delta_{k}=t_{k+1}-t_{k},\qquad Z_{k}\sim_{i.i.d.}\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d}),\qquad k=0,1,\cdots,N-1.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The following theorem guarantees that process (<a href="#S4.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ 4.1 Summary of main results ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) almost surely converges to the target support.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmass1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmass2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
there exists <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> that depends only on <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{0},\gamma,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{0},\gamma,T)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for all <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\kappa\leq\kappa_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\kappa\leq\kappa_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following statement is true:
for any discretization scheme <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{k}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{k}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;t_{k+1}&gt;t_{k}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;t_{k+1}&gt;t_{k}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, for any initialization <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{t_{0}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{t_{0}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and guidance strength <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, almost surely we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lim_{k\to\infty}\mathsf{dist}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}},K_{\eta_{0}})=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗂𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lim_{k\to\infty}\mathsf{dist}(\widehat{x}_{t_{k}},K_{\eta_{0}})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, the sequence <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\widehat{x}_{t_{k}}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\widehat{x}_{t_{k}}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is produced by <a href="#S4.E16" title="In 4.1 Summary of main results ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4.1 Summary of main results ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Proofs for guided DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Intuition: reformulating DDPM with guidance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we reformulate process (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) through a change of variables.
As in the derivations of Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, SDE (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 2.2 Controllable generation with diffusion guidance ‣ 2 Background ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}-\sqrt{2}\mathrm{d}B_{t}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}-\sqrt{2}\mathrm{d}B_{t}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Big\{x_{t}+\frac{2\gamma\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\Big(\overline{m}_{\eta_{0},T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{x_{t}}{\lambda_{T-t}}\Big)-\frac{2(\gamma-1)\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})\Big(\overline{m}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{x_{t}}{\lambda_{T-t}}\Big)\Big\}\mathrm{d}t," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Big\{x_{t}+\frac{2\gamma\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\Big(\overline{m}_{\eta_{0},T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{x_{t}}{\lambda_{T-t}}\Big)-\frac{2(\gamma-1)\lambda_{T-t}}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})\Big(\overline{m}_{\eta,T-t}(x_{t})-\frac{x_{t}}{\lambda_{T-t}}\Big)\Big\}\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where we recall that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{m}_{\eta,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{m}_{\eta,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\zeta}_{\eta,t}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined in <a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.2 Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
Define <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=e^{T-t}x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=e^{T-t}x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>.
With such change of variables, we have</p>
<table id="S4.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\mathrm{d}z_{t}&amp;=\Big\{\frac{2\gamma}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\big({m}_{\eta_{0},T-t}({z_{t}})-{z_{t}}\big)-\frac{2(\gamma-1)}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})\big({m}_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})-z_{t}\big)\Big\}\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}e^{T-t}\mathrm{d}B_{t}\\
&amp;=\Big\{\frac{2\gamma}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}F_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})-\frac{2(\gamma-1)}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})F_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})\Big\}\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}e^{T-t}\mathrm{d}B_{t}.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\mathrm{d}z_{t}&amp;=\Big\{\frac{2\gamma}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\big({m}_{\eta_{0},T-t}({z_{t}})-{z_{t}}\big)-\frac{2(\gamma-1)}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})\big({m}_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})-z_{t}\big)\Big\}\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}e^{T-t}\mathrm{d}B_{t}\\
&amp;=\Big\{\frac{2\gamma}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}F_{\eta_{0},T-t}(z_{t})-\frac{2(\gamma-1)}{\sigma_{T-t}^{2}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{I}}{\zeta}_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})F_{\eta,T-t}(z_{t})\Big\}\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}e^{T-t}\mathrm{d}B_{t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The drift term above is exactly twice that of the ODE in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 3.2 Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).
Consequently, the trajectory defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ 4.2 Intuition: reformulating DDPM with guidance ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) experiences a force that pulls it toward the target support, along with forces that push it away from the other supports. In addition, the process is influenced by a random force arising from the Brownian motion.
When <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently close to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the random force is dominated by the deterministic force, and thus the same intuition in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Intuition: reformulating DDIM with guidance ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> applies.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07220v1/guidance_DDPM.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/168;" width="262" height="168" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of SDE (<a href="#S4.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ 4.2 Intuition: reformulating DDPM with guidance ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) for <math id="S4.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=\{\eta_{0},\eta_{1},\eta_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=\{\eta_{0},\eta_{1},\eta_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As before, the guidance is directed toward the blue circle labeled <math id="S4.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The terms <math id="S4.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote deterministic forces associated with the supports <math id="S4.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\eta_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\eta_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and <math id="S4.F2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm rand}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm rand}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the stochastic force induced by the Brownian motion. The total force is given by <math id="S4.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\rm comb}=F_{0}+F_{1}+F_{2}+F_{\rm rand}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>comb</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\rm comb}=F_{0}+F_{1}+F_{2}+F_{\rm rand}</annotation></semantics></math>. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Numerical experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we illustrate our theoretical results through numerical experiments.
The code used for these experiments is available at <a href="https://github.com/ruijiacao/diffusion_guidance_code" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;">https://github.com/ruijiacao/diffusion_guidance_code</a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Sample trajectories</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1 </span>Example with convex support</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a mixture distribution <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=\frac{1}{3}\mathsf{Unif}\Big(\mathcal{B}\big((-1.5,0.5),\,0.7\big)\Big)+\frac{1}{3}\mathsf{Unif}\Big(\mathcal{B}\big((0.2,3),\,1.2\big)\Big)+\frac{1}{3}\mathsf{Unif}\Big(\mathcal{B}\big((2.5,-0.5),\,1.5\big)\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 0.7</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 1.2</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 1.5</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=\frac{1}{3}\mathsf{Unif}\Big(\mathcal{B}\big((-1.5,0.5),\,0.7\big)\Big)+\frac{1}{3}\mathsf{Unif}\Big(\mathcal{B}\big((0.2,3),\,1.2\big)\Big)+\frac{1}{3}\mathsf{Unif}\Big(\mathcal{B}\big((2.5,-0.5),\,1.5\big)\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a mixture of distributions, each supported on a convex set.
We assume that the guidance is directed toward the second component.
For our experiment, we set <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, and perform the diffusion updates using the exponential integrator scheme, which is defined in <a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> for guided DDIM and in <a href="#S4.E16" title="In 4.1 Summary of main results ‣ 4 Results for DDPM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> for guided DDPM.
We choose the time steps as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle t_{0}=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t_{0}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle t_{k+1}=t_{k}+\Delta_{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t_{k+1}=t_{k}+\Delta_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{k}=\begin{cases}\kappa&amp;\text{if }t_{k}\leq T-1,\\
\frac{\kappa}{(1+\kappa)^{k-M+1}}&amp;\text{if }t_{k}&gt;T-1.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>κ</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mfrac><mi>κ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{k}=\begin{cases}\kappa&amp;\text{if }t_{k}\leq T-1,\\
\frac{\kappa}{(1+\kappa)^{k-M+1}}&amp;\text{if }t_{k}&gt;T-1.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\lfloor(T-1)/\kappa\rfloor+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\lfloor(T-1)/\kappa\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We examine different initial positions and guidance strengths.
The resulting trajectories of the guided DDIM and guided DDPM are shown in Figures <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1.1 Example with convex support ‣ 5.1 Sample trajectories ‣ 5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1.1 Example with convex support ‣ 5.1 Sample trajectories ‣ 5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, respectively.
These figures illustrate that both methods produce sample trajectories that converge to the target support, confirming our theoretical findings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07220v1/ddim_convex_example.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/143;" width="476" height="143"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Trajectories of the guided DDIM under varying guidance strengths and initial positions. The target region is shown in green, the initial position is marked by a blue dot, and the endpoint by an “X”.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07220v1/ddpm_convex_example.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/199;" width="476" height="199"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Trajectories of the guided DDPM under varying guidance strengths and initial positions.
Here, the target region is shown in green, the initial position is marked by a blue dot, and the endpoint by an “X”.
Lighter (darker) colors indicate smaller (larger) time indices within the interval <math id="S5.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T-\delta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T-\delta]</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2 </span>Example with non-convex support</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also provide an example demonstrating that support robustness can continue to hold even in non-convex settings.
As before, we consider mixtures of uniform distributions, with the target support highlighted in green.
Sample trajectories for the guided DDIM and guided DDPM are shown in Figures <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1.2 Example with non-convex support ‣ 5.1 Sample trajectories ‣ 5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1.2 Example with non-convex support ‣ 5.1 Sample trajectories ‣ 5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, respectively.
These figures suggest that (under mild regularity assumptions) our theoretical results could hold even without the convexity assumption, which we defer to future work.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07220v1/ddim_nonconvex_example.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/143;" width="476" height="143"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Trajectories of the guided DDIM for varying guidance strengths and initial positions, with the target distribution being a mixture of uniform distributions over non-convex supports. As before, the target region is highlighted in green, the initial position indicated by a blue dot, and the endpoint marked with an “X.”.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07220v1/ddpm_nonconvex_example.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/199;" width="476" height="199"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Trajectories of the guided DDPM for different guidance strengths and initial positions. As before, lighter (darker) colors correspond to earlier (later) times within the interval <math id="S5.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T-\delta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T-\delta]</annotation></semantics></math>. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Visualization of the guided density</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the second part, we empirically visualize the distributions produced by the guided DDIM and DDPM samplers.
As before, we set <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, and sample <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> points from <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf N}(0,{\rm I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖭</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf N}(0,{\rm I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> as the initial positions.
The target distribution is an equally weighted mixture of five uniform distributions over the shapes shown in Figures <a href="#S5.F7.sf1" title="Figure 7(a) ‣ Figure 7 ‣ 5.2 Visualization of the guided density ‣ 5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F7.sf2" title="Figure 7(b) ‣ Figure 7 ‣ 5.2 Visualization of the guided density ‣ 5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>.
We then run both guided DDIM and DDPM using exponential integrators and plot the resulting samples in the same figures.
These plots show that the distributions generated by diffusion guidance remain concentrated on the target support, further confirming our theoretical findings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07220v1/ddim_densities.png" id="S5.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/130;" width="476" height="130" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Empirical density obtained by running guided DDIM.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07220v1/ddpm_densities.png" id="S5.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/130;" width="476" height="130" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Empirical density obtained by running guided DDPM</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S5.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">In Figures <a href="#S5.F7.sf1" title="Figure 7(a) ‣ Figure 7 ‣ 5.2 Visualization of the guided density ‣ 5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F7.sf2" title="Figure 7(b) ‣ Figure 7 ‣ 5.2 Visualization of the guided density ‣ 5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, we illustrate the empirical densities produced by the guided DDIM and DDPM samplers under varying levels of guidance.
Each colored region represents the empirical distribution obtained by guiding the diffusion model toward its corresponding component.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we show that diffusion models with guidance exhibit the support robustness property.
Our theory applies to both DDIM and DDPM, and encompasses both continuous-time processes and discretizations induced by an exponential integrator.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several interesting directions remain for future exploration:</p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Support robustness without convexity assumptions. </span>
Our numerical experiments in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Numerical experiments ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> suggest that the support robustness property extends to mixture distributions with non-convex support.
We currently impose the convexity assumption due to technical limitations,
removing this restriction and providing a rigorous theoretical justification is an interesting direction for future work.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accommodating score estimation error. </span>
Our current theoretical results assume exact access to the score functions.
As noted in Remark <a href="#S3.Thmrem2" title="Remark 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Summary of main results ‣ 3 Results for DDIM ‣ On the Robustness of Distribution Support under Diffusion Guidance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, extending these results to estimated scores requires controlling the score estimation error under a shifted distribution, which requires additional technical developments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Further analysis of the guided distribution. </span>
This work suggests that diffusion guidance generates samples that remain on the target support.
An interesting open problem is to further characterize the distribution produced by diffusion guidance. In particular, while our results indicate that diffusion guidance produces physically plausible samples, they do not fully explain why it succeeds. A deeper understanding would require a more refined analysis of the guided distribution.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
